-- chair-step: this REVOKEs the implicit PUBLIC EXECUTE from `custom.read_records_matching` and GRANTs it to `authenticated`. A GRANT is refused by the additive allow-list by name, so it comes through this route and a person reads exactly which function and why. The function is the record store's ordinary read door addressed by a QUESTION as well as a page: it resolves the reader from the session (`auth.uid()`), asks `custom.assert_may_know_table` exactly as `custom.read_records` does on its second line, takes its level from `custom.effective_level`, its visible columns from `iam.visible_field_ids`, and its rows from `custom.visible_predicate_sql` — the ONE ladder, written into this door's own WHERE — so it can only ever answer with records the caller could already have paged to. The filter NARROWS that set and has no arm that can widen it: its keys are refused by shape by `custom.agg_assert_key` before they reach `format`, its values are quoted literals, a window's moments are cast to timestamptz before the statement is built, and it is evaluated by `custom.record_filter_sql` — the same body `custom.agg_sql` calls, so the rows this door opens are the rows the number on the dashboard counted. Field masking, hidden-field notices, choice rendering and the page ceiling are the same calls the page door makes. It exists because a dashboard number could not be walked into: a bar said 27, the click opened the whole table, and a sentence underneath apologised for it. The REVOKE NARROWS: it takes PUBLIC off before `authenticated` is named. No DROP, no data movement, no existing declared grant changed.
-- lane: DRILL (chair ruling 2026-09-21: a new name cannot collide)
--
--   custom.read_records_matching(uuid, uuid, jsonb, boolean, integer, integer)  → authenticated, EXECUTE

revoke all on function custom.read_records_matching(uuid, uuid, jsonb, boolean, integer, integer) from public;

grant execute on function custom.read_records_matching(uuid, uuid, jsonb, boolean, integer, integer) to authenticated;
